diagnosis_text:
The network is completely healthy. All components that the ticket hypothesises as faulty were directly verified to be operational. R1's two WAN uplinks are both up and holding valid DHCP addresses from their respective ISPs: eth2 (203.0.113.2/30, facing ISPA) and eth3 (203.0.113.6/30, facing ISPB). R1 has an active kernel default route via 203.0.113.1 (ISPA) and is redistributing it into OSPF via `default-information originate`. All interior routers — csw1, csw2, dswa1, dswa2, dswb1, dswb2 — are receiving the OSPF-originated default route with ECMP paths through both core switches. NAT masquerade rules for every internal subnet (10.1–10.6.0.0/24) are installed on R1 and were observed actively processing packets (17 hits / 1044 bytes on the 10.1.0.0/24 → eth2 rule after a single ping session from PC1). End-to-end Internet reachability was confirmed from three different Office A devices: PC1 (10.1.0.100) reached 172.253.62.100, 66.235.200.145 (ISPA loopbacks), and 216.239.35.4 (ISPB loopback) with 0% packet loss; pc2 and laptop1 both reached the ISPA gateway (203.0.113.1) without loss. The ISP RFC1918 ingress filters on ISPA and ISPB are correctly configured security policy and do not affect post-NAT Internet traffic. The root cause of the ticket is a false premise — there is no Internet outage; the R1 uplinks and ISP peers are fully operational and Office A users have uninterrupted Internet connectivity. No corrective action is required.
=== END ===